+Two execution semantics must be distinguished. Algorithm~\ref{alg:inferencia}
+as written is the persistent-state deployment target: one recurrent step per
+executed update, and suppressed frames are never processed. The evaluation
+protocol of Section~\ref{sec:protocolo} implements each executed update by
+recomputing the causal prefix window of the last $W{=}96$ available frames
+(left-padded; a verification script accompanies the artifact), so an executed
+update costs $W$ recurrent steps and later updates re-read the inputs of
+previously suppressed frames. The two semantics agree on FR under the fixed
+policy (identical detections; $\mathrm{TTD}_b$ differs by $6$\,ms on
+seed~42, a left-padding warm-up effect), but they are \emph{not} equivalent
+under suppression: with the same seed-42 thresholds, the evaluated
+recompute-on-update semantics yields $\mathrm{TTD}_{\mathrm{ef}} = 0.023$\,s
+at $44.7\%$ suppression, whereas literal single-step skipping yields
+$0.170$\,s at $44.4\%$. All scheduled results in this paper therefore refer
+to the recompute-on-update semantics, whose per-frame compute is
+$c_{\mathrm{gate}} + (1-s)\,W\,\mathcal{O}(4(DH{+}H^2)+8H^2)$ and whose
+measured ms/frame figures (Tables~\ref{tab:resultados_principais}
+and~\ref{tab:deployment}) are wall-clock times of this simulation; deploying
+the single-step variant of Algorithm~\ref{alg:inferencia} with its
+lower per-update cost would require recalibrating the thresholds and
+re-evaluating detection under that semantics, which we have not done.}
